From: enij90 <226572015+enij90@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/msm/dpu: don't wedge the CTL when tearing down a video encoder

dpu_encoder_helper_phys_cleanup() ends with a CTL flush plus CTL_START,
and resets and flushes the merge_3d block of the pingpong unconditionally.
For a video mode encoder both leave the CTL stuck:

- A video mode CTL is never started (phys_vid has no trigger_start op);
  its flush latches on the next frame of the timing engine. Cleanup runs
  after the timing engine has been disabled, and CTL_START makes the CTL
  process the flush immediately. The INTF and DSC parts of it can never
  complete without a running timing engine.

- The PP carries its merge_3d for every topology, including the ones that
  keep it out of the CTL path, such as dual-DSI with DSC merge
  (2 LM -> 2 DSC -> 2 INTF, mode_3d = NONE). The enable paths (video,
  writeback) program and flush merge_3d only when mode_3d is set. A flush
  of a merge_3d that is not active in the CTL never completes either.

The CTL then keeps that flush in progress (on DPU 10 visible at CTL+0x94,
with the merge_3d/DSC/INTF sub-masks at CTL+0x180..0x18c; CTL_SW_RESET
does not clear it) and does not take any later flush until the MDSS power
collapses. A mode change keeps the DPU powered between disable and enable,
so the first commit on the same CTL is never latched: the SSPP does not
fetch, the DSC is not started and the panel stays black with the backlight
on. A DPMS cycle recovers only because the runtime suspend resets the CTL.

Seen on the Lenovo Legion Tab Gen 3 (SM8650, dual-DSI panel with DSC) on
every refresh rate switch. With the flush left pending instead of started,
it latches together with the first flush of the next enable.

Skip CTL_START in the cleanup of video mode encoders, and gate the
merge_3d reset, its flush and intf_cfg.merge_3d on mode_3d, as the enable
paths do.

---
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_encoder.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_encoder.c
@@ -2313,11 +2313,14 @@
 {
 	struct dpu_hw_ctl *ctl = phys_enc->hw_ctl;
 	struct dpu_hw_intf_cfg intf_cfg = { 0 };
+	enum dpu_3d_blend_mode mode_3d;
 	int i;
 	struct dpu_encoder_virt *dpu_enc;
 
 	dpu_enc = to_dpu_encoder_virt(phys_enc->parent);
 
+	mode_3d = dpu_encoder_helper_get_3d_blend_mode(phys_enc);
+
 	ctl->ops.reset(ctl);
 
 	dpu_encoder_helper_reset_mixers(phys_enc);
@@ -2357,8 +2360,13 @@
 	if (dpu_enc->cwb_mask)
 		dpu_encoder_helper_phys_setup_cwb(phys_enc, false);
 
-	/* reset the merge 3D HW block */
-	if (phys_enc->hw_pp && phys_enc->hw_pp->merge_3d) {
+	/*
+	 * Reset the merge 3D HW block only if it was in use, like the enable
+	 * paths. The PP carries its merge_3d also for topologies that leave
+	 * it out of the CTL path (e.g. DSC merge), and a flush of a merge_3d
+	 * that is not active in the CTL never completes.
+	 */
+	if (mode_3d && phys_enc->hw_pp && phys_enc->hw_pp->merge_3d) {
 		phys_enc->hw_pp->merge_3d->ops.setup_3d_mode(phys_enc->hw_pp->merge_3d,
 				BLEND_3D_NONE);
 		if (ctl->ops.update_pending_flush_merge_3d)
@@ -2381,7 +2389,7 @@
 	}
 
 	intf_cfg.stream_sel = 0; /* Ignored by reset_intf_cfg */
-	intf_cfg.mode_3d = dpu_encoder_helper_get_3d_blend_mode(phys_enc);
+	intf_cfg.mode_3d = mode_3d;
 	intf_cfg.dsc = dpu_encoder_helper_get_dsc(phys_enc);
 	intf_cfg.cwb = dpu_enc->cwb_mask;
 
@@ -2390,14 +2398,21 @@
 	if (phys_enc->hw_wb)
 		intf_cfg.wb = phys_enc->hw_wb->idx;
 
-	if (phys_enc->hw_pp && phys_enc->hw_pp->merge_3d)
+	if (mode_3d && phys_enc->hw_pp && phys_enc->hw_pp->merge_3d)
 		intf_cfg.merge_3d = phys_enc->hw_pp->merge_3d->idx;
 
 	if (ctl->ops.reset_intf_cfg)
 		ctl->ops.reset_intf_cfg(ctl, &intf_cfg);
 
 	ctl->ops.trigger_flush(ctl);
-	ctl->ops.trigger_start(ctl);
+	/*
+	 * A video mode CTL is never started: its flush latches on the next
+	 * frame of the timing engine, which is off here. CTL_START makes the
+	 * CTL process the flush right away instead, and with the timing
+	 * engine stopped the INTF/DSC parts of it never complete.
+	 */
+	if (phys_enc->intf_mode != INTF_MODE_VIDEO)
+		ctl->ops.trigger_start(ctl);
 	ctl->ops.clear_pending_flush(ctl);
 }
 
